{% extends "base.jinja" %}
{% block body %}
    <div class="container nt-about">
        <h2>About newtrackon.co.nz</h2>
<p>This is a New Zealand (Wellington) mirror of newTrackon, a service that monitors open/public BitTorrent trackers and publishes
which ones actually work. It runs the upstream code with patches that change how trackers are checked, scored and retired, plus
test clients on three continents. Everything below describes how <em>this</em> site works.</p>

<h4>1. How a tracker is checked</h4>
<p>Every check is a real announce, the same request a BitTorrent client sends, made from Wellington:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>UDP trackers</b> (BEP 15): a <em>connect</em> request, then an <em>announce</em> with the returned connection ID. Each step has a
10-second timeout and the exchange is tried twice. If the tracker publishes both IPv4 and IPv6, the retry uses the other family, so one
dead address can't make a working tracker look down.</li>
<li><b>HTTP/HTTPS trackers</b>: a standard announce with a 10-second timeout (every published address is tried).</li>
<li>A check passes only on a valid, well-formed announce response. Timeouts, refusals, error replies and garbage all fail.</li>
<li>A BEP 34 DNS TXT record, if published, sets the preferred protocol and port.</li>
<li><b>Peer test</b>: after each successful check, a second client (new peer ID, different port, as a leecher) announces the same random
torrent <b>from a different IP address</b>: one of three VPN exits, in Europe, North America and Asia. Exits are tried in random order,
starting with the one that last answered that tracker, and each exit moves to a different server every 6 hours, so a tracker can't dodge
the test by blocking one address. A tracker that really tracks peers hands the first client to the second. If all three exits are ignored,
a second client from this server is tried: it can prove a pass (only if the tracker kept the two clients separate and dropped the first
when it left), but a miss from the same IP proves nothing, so it never counts as a fail. The verdict uses the <b>last 6 conclusive
tests</b>: <b>3 or more fails sets the score to 0</b> and the status to <b>Up/Bad</b>. This catches trackers that answer every announce but
never hand out peers, or only sometimes do because their servers don't share data.</li>
<li><b>Fake-peer test</b>: the test torrent is a random one only this site knows, so the only real peers are our own test clients. Any
other peer in the reply was fake (anti-piracy decoys, stale junk). <b>3 checks in a row sets the score to 0</b> (Up/Bad).</li>
<li><b>Dual-stack test</b>: every IP family a tracker publishes (IPv4 and/or IPv6) is tried each check, from this server and then from every
VPN exit. A family counts as dead only if <em>nobody</em> gets an answer on it, so one lossy path can't fake it. A dead published address
breaks the tracker for every client on that family (most clients are IPv4-only). <b>3 dead checks in a row caps the score at 50</b> (Up/Broken: it still works over the other family).</li>
<li><b>Count check</b>: the true seeder/leecher count for the random torrent is 1 / 1. Reporting more is inflating. Flagged, no penalty.</li>
<li><b>Stopped test</b>: the first client announces &ldquo;stopped&rdquo; and the second asks again. A well-run tracker drops the departed
peer at once. Flagged, no penalty. Both clients then leave, so no test peers are left behind.</li>
<li><b>Spoofing test</b> (UDP): an announce with a made-up connection ID must be refused (BEP 15). A tracker that accepts it can be abused
to aim swarm traffic at a third party. Shield, no penalty. HTTP/HTTPS trackers always get the green shield (TCP can't be spoofed).</li>
<li>Hostnames resolving to non-public addresses (private, reserved, multicast, site-local) are removed immediately.</li>
<li><b>Monitor self-check</b>: before recording any failure, the monitor confirms it has working internet and DNS itself. If it doesn't,
the outage is ours: nothing is recorded, no tracker is penalised or removed, and checks resume when the connection is back. If a check fails from Wellington but the tracker answers through the VPN exits, it counts as half an outage: down for NZ, up for the rest of the world. If a name won't resolve here but public DNS (Cloudflare, Quad9) knows it, the fault is our resolver's: nothing is recorded.</li>
</ul>

<h4>2. Latency</h4>
<p>The Latency column is the average of the per-region figures below (see Latency and score at the end of this page). A dash means the tracker is down.</p>
<p><b>Latency by region</b> (hover the figure, or open the row): one request round trip to the tracker from Wellington (Oceania) and from each VPN
exit's region (currently in Europe, North America and Asia; the cities change as the exits rotate). The time through our own VPN tunnel is
measured separately and subtracted, so each figure is the tracker's distance from that city, accurate to roughly &plusmn;10&ndash;20 ms. For HTTP/HTTPS trackers the TCP and TLS setup is done before the clock starts, so each figure is one request round trip; for trackers behind a CDN that includes the hop to the real server, not just the nearest edge. Each region's figure is the median of its last 12 measurements within 48 hours. Latency does affect the score: see Latency and score at the end of this page.</p>

<p>The API's <code>fast_from</code> filter uses these figures to list trackers that are quick from one part of the world (see The API below).</p>
<h4>3. Check history</h4>
<p>Results are kept as a timeline of <b>30-minute slots</b>, each up or down. A check fills in every slot since the previous one: same
result as before fills them all; a changed result splits the gap down the middle; two checks in one slot keep the worse. The history is a
picture of time, not a count of checks, and is never longer than the time since the tracker was added.</p>

<h4>4. The score (out of 100)</h4>
<p>The score is <b>not</b> an uptime percentage. It combines two things, both weighted towards recent history:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Availability</b>: the share of slots the tracker was up, each weighted by age with a <b>half-life of 7 days</b>.</li>
<li><b>Stability</b>: how often it flips between up and down (age-weighted flip rate); stability = (1 &minus; flip rate)&sup2;.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Score = availability &times; stability &times; 100.</b> One clean outage costs little; constant flapping is punished hard, because an
unreliable tracker is nearly as bad as a dead one. Slow trackers then lose points for latency (see Latency and score at the end of this page). A new tracker's score is capped at 80, rising to 100 over its first 7 days of history, so a top score has to be earned.</p>
<table class="table table-sm" style="max-width:640px">
<thead><tr><th>Behaviour (two weeks)</th><th>Availability</th><th>Score</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Up the whole time</td><td>100%</td><td>100</td></tr>
<tr><td>One 6-hour outage two days ago</td><td>97%</td><td>96</td></tr>
<tr><td>One failed half-hour every day</td><td>98%</td><td>91</td></tr>
<tr><td>Randomly failing 1 check in 10</td><td>91%</td><td>66</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flapping up/down every hour</td><td>50%</td><td>13</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><b>Quality penalties</b> (the harshest applies; each lifts as soon as the tracker behaves again):</p>
<table class="table table-sm" style="max-width:640px">
<thead><tr><th>Fault</th><th>Rule</th><th>Score</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Hands out no peers</td><td>3+ fails in its last 6 peer tests</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fake peers</td><td>3 checks in a row</td><td>set to 0</td></tr>
<tr><td>A published IP family is dead</td><td>3 checks in a row</td><td>capped at 50</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>A penalised tracker shows <b>Up/Bad</b> (or <b>Up/Broken</b> if its only problem is a dead IP family) and drops out of every score-based API list. Counts survive restarts.</p>
<p><b>Stars</b>: ★★★★★ 90+ · ★★★★ 70–89 · ★★★ 50–69 · ★★ 30–49 · ★ 10–29 · none below 10. Scores are shown rounded, and every threshold on the site uses that
rounded value.</p>

<h4>5. How often trackers are checked</h4>
<p>Intervals adapt like NTP polling: proven trackers are checked less often, misbehaving ones closely. All intervals are multiples of
30 minutes apart from the 15-minute watch rate.</p>
<table class="table table-sm">
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Checked every</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Newly added (under 3 slots of history)</td><td>30 min</td></tr>
<tr><td>Up, but had any failure in the last 6 hours</td><td>15 min</td></tr>
<tr><td>Up and clean for the last 6 hours</td><td>+30 min after each success, up to <b>60 min</b></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Premium: score 95+ (before the latency penalty) and a clean record that grows with the interval</td><td><b>90 min</b> after 12 h clean &middot; <b>2 h</b> after 24 h &middot;
<b>2 h 30</b> after 30 h &middot; <b>3 h</b> after 36 h &middot; <b>3 h 30</b> after 42 h &middot; <b>4 h</b> after 48 h</td></tr>
<tr><td>Just went down, or flapping</td><td>15 min</td></tr>
<tr><td>Down for more than 6 hours</td><td>60 min, then +30 min per failed check, up to <b>4 h</b></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>A premium tracker that fails, or whose score (before the latency penalty) drops below 95, loses premium at once. Each check is scheduled at a random point within
&plusmn;10% of its interval so checks don't land in lockstep; the Interval column shows the nominal value.</p>

<h4>6. The main list</h4>
<ul>
<li>Rows are navy; a tracker that is <b>down right now</b> has a black row. Quality is shown by the score, stars and status, not the row colour. Text turns grey for trackers scoring under 90 (not on the stable list), except Up/New ones, which are only held back by their age.</li>
<li><b>Latency</b>, <b>Score</b> and stars: sections 2 and 4.</li>
<li><b>Last 7 days</b>: one dot per day; <b>Last 24 hours: one dot per 2 hours.</b> Green up, red if any check in it failed, grey not monitored yet.</li>
<li><b>Status</b>: <b style="color:#00e676">Up/Good</b> (answering, passes every test, score 90+) · Up/Slow (reliable, but averaging 300 ms or more, or under 90 only because of the latency penalty at 200 ms or more) · Up/Unreliable (up, but under 90 because of dropouts) · Up/Broken (answers, but one of its published IP families is dead) · Up/New (healthy, but still building its first 7 days of history) · Up/Junk (score under 50) · Up/Bad (answering but fails a quality test; hover for which) &middot; <b style="color:#ff3b30">Down</b>, with how long (e.g. &ldquo;3d 14h&rdquo;, &ldquo;7h&rdquo;, &ldquo;25m&rdquo;).
Then the main mark: &#10008; down &middot; &#9760; fake peers (red: 3 in a row) &middot; &#9888; peer test failed (red: 3+ of last 6) &middot;
&#10004; peer test passed. Then the IP families: <b style="color:#28a745">IPv4</b> answers, <b style="color:#dc3545">IPv4</b> confirmed dead,
<b style="color:#555">IPv6</b> not published. Then flags (no penalty): &#9650; inflated counts &middot; &#8856; keeps departed peers &middot; shield
(green spoof-proof, red spoofable, grey not tested yet; none on down trackers).</li>
<li><b>&#10697;N</b> after a URL: this tracker and N&minus;1 others share an operator: the same servers (current or recent IPs) or the same domain.
Adding several of them to a torrent gains little.</li>
<li><b>Recent events</b> (below the table): the last 10 state changes, with a link to the feed (section 7).</li>
<li><b>&#9654; expand a row</b> for IP addresses, country, network, date added, <b>Stats</b> (availability over 7 days and all time, outage count,
longest outage, recent peer-test record, the tracker's own announce interval, latency by region) and <b>Same operator</b>.</li>
<li><b>Checked</b> ticks live; <b>Update interval</b> is the current nominal interval. The table refreshes itself every 2 minutes, keeping your
sort, page and position.</li>
</ul>

<h4>7. The API</h4>
<ul>
<li><code>/api/stable</code> (and <code>/api/best</code>): score 90+, up right now, listed for at least 7 days.</li>
<li><code>/api/&lt;percentage&gt;</code>, e.g. <code>/api/90</code>: score at or above that value and up right now.</li>
<li><code>/api/udp</code>, <code>/api/http</code>: score 90+ and up right now, by protocol.</li>
<li><code>/api/live</code>: everything that passed its latest check. <code>/api/all</code>: every tracker monitored, including down ones.</li>
<li><code>/feed.xml</code>: an Atom/RSS feed of events: a tracker going <b>down</b>, coming back <b>up</b> (and after how long), turning Up/Bad, Up/Broken, Up/Slow, Up/Unreliable or Up/Junk (with the reason) or Up/Good again, an IP family confirmed dead or answering again, trackers <b>added</b> and <b>removed</b>. Down/up churn of trackers scoring under 50 isn't logged. Subscribe in any feed reader, or a Discord/Telegram RSS bot. Filters: <code>?tracker=example.org</code> (one tracker) and <code>?type=down,up,bad,good,family,added,removed</code>. Example: <code>/feed.xml?tracker=opentrackr&amp;type=down,bad</code></li>
<li><code>/api/clean</code>: the list to put in your client. <b>Up/Good or Up/Slow (never Junk, Bad, Broken or down), score 90+, listed at least 3 days, no confirmed dead IP family, and one tracker per operator (the best of each group).</b> Options: <code>min_score</code> (default 90), <code>min_age_days</code> (default 3), <code>max_latency</code> (ms, region average), <code>require_ipv4=true</code>, <code>dedupe=false</code><code></code>. Example: <code>/api/clean?max_latency=500&amp;require_ipv4=true</code></li>
<li><code>/api/details</code>: JSON with everything this page knows about every tracker: status and problems, score, latency and latency by region,
IP families, peer-test record, fake-peer streak, flags, the tracker's announce interval, stats, same-operator group, IPs, country, network and region.</li>
<li><b>Region filters</b>, on every list above (and <code>/api/clean</code> and <code>/api/details</code>). Regions follow how the internet is wired rather than
the map: <code>americas</code> (North, Central and South America: South America mostly reaches the world through North America),
<code>europe</code> (Europe, Russia, Central Asia, the Middle East and Africa: their traffic is mostly exchanged in European hubs) and
<code>asia-pacific</code> (Asia and Oceania).
<ul>
<li><code>region=</code>: trackers <b>located in</b> those regions, by the country of their IP addresses (comma-separated, e.g. <code>region=americas,europe</code>).</li>
<li><code>fast_from=</code>: trackers that <b>answer quickly from</b> one of the four places latency is measured from:
<code>americas</code> (North America), <code>europe</code>, <code>asia</code> or <code>oceania</code> (Wellington): under 150&nbsp;ms by default,
change it with <code>fast_from_ms=</code>. Asia and Oceania are separate here because no tracker is close to both: from NZ, Asia is often
reached via the USA. These are measured round trips, so they follow real internet routing. Trackers not yet measured from there are left out.</li>
</ul>
Examples: <code>/api/clean?fast_from=oceania</code> (the clean list, quick from Australia and NZ), <code>/api/stable?fast_from=asia</code>,
<code>/api/stable?region=europe</code>, <code>/api/clean?fast_from=americas&amp;fast_from_ms=100</code>. The <a href="/list">list page</a> has the same choices.</li>
</ul>
<p>Penalised (Up/Bad or Up/Broken) trackers drop out of every score-based list until they behave again.</p>

<h4>8. Removal and bans</h4>
<p>Flappers stay listed so you can see them. A tracker is removed after at least 3 days listed if it has had no passed check in 5 days, or if its availability × stability is under 15% (it answers too rarely or drops out too often to be useful). Removed hosts are auto-banned for <b>30 days</b>, then
may be resubmitted; hosts banned by hand stay banned.</p>

